<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="so6" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>SO6: Applications of Second-Order Differential Equations</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can solve applied problems resulting from second-order differential equations.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <subsection xml:id="sec-second-order-apps-harmonic">
        <title>Harmonic Motion</title>
        
        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In this example, we will develop the equation for free damped motion in a mass-spring-damper system.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Suppose that we have a mass attached to a spring (with sprint constant <m>k</m>) which moves in a straight line. Further suppose that the mass is connected to some sort of shock absorber, called a damper, which has coefficient <m>c</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Newton's second law of motion tells us that 
                    <md>
                        F = ma
                    </md>
                    If we consider a position function <m>y(x)</m>, then we can recast Newton's second law as
                    <md>
                        F = my''
                    </md>
                    In the spring system, we also must consider Hooke's law:
                    <md>
                        F = ky
                    </md>
                    Since the spring force opposes the direction of motion, we have
                    <md>
                        my'' = -ky
                    </md>
                    If we further assume that the damping constant is nonzero, and that damping is proportional to velocity, we have that
                    <md>
                        my'' = -ky - cy'
                    </md>
                    or
                    <md>
                        my'' + cy' + ky = 0
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    The equation <m>my'' + cy' + ky = 0</m> can be rewritten as
                    <md>
                        y'' + 2\lambda y' + \omega^2 y = 0
                    </md>
                    where <m>\lambda = \dfrac{c}{2m}</m> and <m>\omega = \sqrt{k/m}</m>. 
                </p>
                <p>
                    The equation represents <em>free</em> motion because we assume no forcing term; <term>forced harmonic motion</term> occurs when we have a forcing term on the right hand side (generally in terms of cosine).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <definition xml:id="def-over-critical-under-damped">
            <title>Overdamped/Critically Damped/Underdamped Systems</title>
            <idx><h>overdamped system</h></idx>
            <idx><h>critically damped system</h></idx>
            <idx><h>underdamped system</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider a mass-spring-damper system with mass <m>m</m>, damping coefficient <m>c</m>, and spring constant <m>k</m>, modeled by the equation
                    <md>
                        y'' + 2\lambda y' +\omega^2 y = 0
                    </md>
                    with <m>\lambda = \dfrac{c}{2m}</m> and <m>\omega = \sqrt{k/m}</m>. 
                </p>
                <p>
                    The system is said to be <term>overdamped</term> if <m>\lamba^2 \gt \omega^2</m>, <term>critically damped</term> if <m>\lambda^2 = \omega^2</m>, and <term>underdamped</term> if <m>\lambda^2 \lt \omega^2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <remark>
            <p>
                To be overdamped means that the damping coefficient is large compared to the spring constant, which results 
            </p>
        </remark>
